---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import Layers from '../../components/Layers.astro';
import { scenes } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'The Angles', href: '/explore/angles/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Angles — Ascendant, Descendant, Midheaven, and IC"
  description="The four angles are the most time-sensitive points in a chart: the Ascendant, Descendant, Midheaven, and Imum Coeli. What each one marks and why they need your exact birth time."
  path="/explore/angles/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>The four angles</h1>
        <p class="lede">Four points define the frame every other placement sits inside. They are also the reason your birth time matters more than your birth date.</p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.birthChartBasics.src}
        alt={scenes.birthChartBasics.alt}
        width={scenes.birthChartBasics.width}
        height={scenes.birthChartBasics.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <Layers kind="astronomy">
        <p>
          The angles come from the intersection of two circles: the ecliptic (the Sun's apparent
          path) and the local horizon and meridian at your exact place and moment of birth. Because
          the Earth rotates once every 24 hours, all four angles move through the entire zodiac
          every day — roughly one degree every four minutes. This is why a four-minute error in
          birth time is a real error, and a four-hour one changes the chart completely.
        </p>
      </Layers>

      <h2>The Ascendant (ASC) — the first house cusp</h2>
      <p>
        The zodiac degree rising over the eastern horizon at your birth. It is the chart's starting
        point: it determines which sign begins the first house and, from there, the entire house
        structure. Read for temperament, physical presence, and the instinctive manner you lead
        with. Commonly called the <a href="/explore/the-big-three/">rising sign</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The Descendant (DSC) — the seventh house cusp</h2>
      <p>
        Exactly opposite the Ascendant, and setting in the west as you were born. It marks the
        <a href="/explore/houses/seventh-house/">seventh house</a> of partnership: what you seek in
        others, what you meet in close one-to-one relationships, and — in modern practice — what you
        tend to project outward rather than own.
      </p>

      <h2>The Midheaven (MC) — the tenth house cusp</h2>
      <p>
        The highest point the ecliptic reaches in your chart, from the Latin <em>Medium Coeli</em>,
        "middle of the sky." It marks the <a href="/explore/houses/tenth-house/">tenth house</a> of
        career, reputation, and public direction — what you are visible for, and what you climb
        toward over a working life.
      </p>

      <h2>The Imum Coeli (IC) — the fourth house cusp</h2>
      <p>
        Directly opposite the Midheaven — <em>Imum Coeli</em>, "bottom of the sky," the point
        beneath your feet at birth. It marks the <a href="/explore/houses/fourth-house/">fourth
        house</a> of home, family, roots, and the private self. Where the MC is what the world sees,
        the IC is what only the household knows.
      </p>

      <h2>Why they carry extra weight</h2>
      <p>
        Angles are not planets — they are positions in space. But a planet sitting close to one is
        considered significantly amplified: an angular planet expresses itself loudly and visibly in
        a life. Traditional astrology formalizes this as
        <a href="/explore/dignities/">accidental dignity</a>, and it's one of the fastest ways to
        find what dominates a chart.
      </p>
      <p>
        The two axes together — ASC/DSC and MC/IC — form a cross that organizes the whole chart into
        self versus other, and public versus private. Almost every question a client brings sits
        somewhere on that cross.
      </p>

      <Layers kind="differs">
        <p>
          The Ascendant and Midheaven are fixed by astronomy, but how the intermediate house cusps
          are derived from them varies by <a href="/explore/house-systems/">house system</a>. In
          whole sign houses the Midheaven may fall in the ninth, tenth, or eleventh house rather
          than defining the tenth cusp — which surprises people moving between systems.
        </p>
      </Layers>

      <div class="callout">
        <p style="margin: 0;">
          <strong>Don't know your birth time?</strong> The angles are what you lose without it —
          the planets remain, but the frame goes. <a href="/prepare-for-your-reading/">Here's how to
          track it down.</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand heading="Your angles are personal to the minute" body="That's the part of a chart no app can guess — and where a reading starts." buttonLabel="About the Natal Reading" buttonHref="/readings/natal/" />
</BaseLayout>
